CAT = "uksiaPhysical"; SEG = "PHY"
# Unit 4, LO1 to LO3 (the MCQ part): implications, risks, reducing risk; LO4 and LO5 practical terms as background.
Q = [
("What is a physical intervention?",
 "Using force to limit someone's movement", ["Giving a customer a verbal warning", "Searching a customer's bag", "Refusing entry at the door"],
 "A physical intervention is the use of direct or indirect force, by bodily, physical or mechanical means, to limit another person's movement."),
("What are defensive physical skills used for?",
 "Protecting yourself from assault", ["Moving a customer out of the venue", "Holding someone on the floor", "Searching a customer for weapons"],
 "Defensive physical skills protect you from assault, for example by evading blows or breaking free from a grab. Physical interventions limit someone else's movement."),
("When should physical intervention be used?",
 "As a last resort", ["Whenever a customer swears", "Before speaking to anyone", "To speed up closing time"],
 "Physical intervention is a last resort, used only when communication and other options have failed or are likely to fail."),
("Any force used by a door supervisor must be:",
 "Reasonable and necessary", ["Enough to end all resistance", "Approved by a manager first", "Equal to the force used on you"],
 "Force must be reasonable, necessary and proportionate in the circumstances. Matching force for force or overpowering someone can easily become unlawful."),
("Which of these most often prevents the need for physical intervention?",
 "Good conflict management", ["Strong grip strength", "Knowing pressure points", "Fast reaction speed"],
 "Calm communication and conflict management resolve most situations without any force, which is why the conflict management unit is taught before physical intervention. Physical skills are for when those fail."),
("What does non-pain compliance mean?",
 "Gaining control without causing pain", ["Using pressure points to gain control", "Holding on until they stop moving", "Using pain only as a last resort"],
 "Licence-linked training teaches non-pain compliant holds and escorts, which control a person without deliberately causing pain."),
("Which is an individual risk factor that makes restraint more dangerous?",
 "Recent alcohol or drug use", ["A well-lit, open space", "Several trained staff present", "A short standing hold"],
 "Individual factors include age, size, weight, medical conditions, exhaustion, recent food, alcohol or drugs, and mental health. Open space and trained staff reduce risk."),
("Which of these is a situational risk factor in a physical intervention?",
 "A confined space", ["The person's age", "The person's weight", "A medical condition"],
 "Situational factors are about the setting: confined or open space, hazards, staff numbers, available help and threats from others. Age, weight and health are individual factors."),
("What is positional asphyxia?",
 "Breathing restricted by body position", ["Choking on food or vomit", "Fainting after a heavy blow to the head", "A panic attack during restraint"],
 "Positional or restraint asphyxia happens when the way someone is held stops them breathing properly. People have died from it in the UK during forceful restraint."),
("Which position carries the highest risk of positional asphyxia?",
 "Held face down on the ground", ["Standing in an escort hold", "Sitting upright on a chair", "Walking with a guiding hand"],
 "Most restraint-related deaths follow forceful ground restraint, especially face down, where weight on the torso restricts breathing."),
("Why is bending a seated person forward during restraint dangerous?",
 "It can restrict their breathing", ["It can dislocate their shoulder", "It makes escorting them harder", "It raises their blood pressure"],
 "Holding someone bent forward while seated, or forced over against a wall, compresses the chest and can cause positional asphyxia."),
("How does the length of a restraint affect risk?",
 "Longer restraint means higher risk", ["Risk falls once they stop struggling", "Length makes no difference", "Risk is highest only at the start"],
 "The longer someone is held, the longer they are exposed to risk, including positional asphyxia and other complications. End restraint at the earliest opportunity."),
("Which is a sign of acute behavioural disturbance?",
 "High temperature and bizarre behaviour", ["Slurred speech and drowsiness", "Calm, slow and quiet speech", "Shivering, with very pale and cold skin"],
 "Acute behavioural disturbance combines signs such as high temperature, bizarre behaviour, exhaustion and abnormal strength. It can lead to sudden death."),
("How should suspected acute behavioural disturbance be treated?",
 "As a medical emergency", ["As a public order offence", "As ordinary drunkenness", "As grounds for arrest"],
 "Acute behavioural disturbance and psychosis can cause sudden death, so treat them as medical emergencies, use calming techniques and get medical help."),
("If a person goes to the ground during an intervention, what should staff do?",
 "Get them up or seated quickly", ["Hold them down until police come", "Kneel on their back for control", "Let go and walk away at once"],
 "Ground restraint carries extra risk. Get the person up, seated or into the recovery position as soon as possible and monitor their breathing meanwhile."),
("During a restraint on the ground, what is the team leader's job?",
 "Take charge of the person's safety", ["Hold the person's legs and feet still", "Fetch the venue manager", "Film the incident on a phone"],
 "A designated team leader takes responsibility for the person's safety, keeps talking to them, monitors them or puts a colleague at the head to do so, and ends the restraint as early as possible."),
("Which restraint method should always be avoided?",
 "Holds on the neck", ["Escorting by the arm", "A guiding hand on the back", "A standing two-person hold"],
 "Neck holds and anything affecting the neck, spine or vital organs can impair breathing or circulation and cause serious injury or death."),
("A person being restrained says 'I can't breathe'. What should you do?",
 "Take it seriously and release", ["Ignore it, as they can talk", "Hold on until they calm down", "Tighten the hold briefly"],
 "People can still speak while suffering positional asphyxia. Act on any complaint of breathing difficulty: release, assess and get medical help."),
("Which of these is a red flag during a restraint?",
 "Blue tinge around the lips", ["Swearing at staff", "Pulling an arm away", "Asking to be let go"],
 "Red flags include difficulty breathing, blueness of the lips, face or nails, vomiting, reduced consciousness, high temperature and confusion. Release and get medical help."),
("A person is unconscious after an incident but breathing normally. What should you do?",
 "Use the recovery position", ["Start chest compressions", "Sit them up on a chair", "Leave them to sleep it off"],
 "Someone unconscious but breathing normally, with no other life-threatening condition, goes in the recovery position and is monitored. CPR is for someone not breathing normally."),
("When should CPR be started?",
 "When they are not breathing normally", ["As soon as someone faints", "When their breathing is slow", "When they are very drunk and fast asleep"],
 "Start CPR when a person is unresponsive and not breathing, or not breathing normally. Occasional gasping breaths are not normal breathing and still need CPR."),
("What do the ABC checks stand for when monitoring a person being held?",
 "Airway, breathing, circulation", ["Alertness, bleeding, consciousness", "Age, build, condition", "Arms, back, chest"],
 "Make sure nothing stops the person breathing or affects their circulation by checking airway, breathing and circulation throughout."),
("What should you tell paramedics who take over after a restraint?",
 "The method and length of restraint", ["The venue's admissions and dress policy", "Only your SIA licence number", "The person's bar bill"],
 "Brief the emergency services on what you know about the person, the risk behaviour, the method and duration of restraint, and any concerns for their wellbeing."),
("Who must write a report after a physical intervention?",
 "Every member of staff involved", ["Only the team leader", "Only the person who started it", "Only the venue manager"],
 "All staff involved must complete a full report, each accounting for their own actions, the force used, injuries and aftercare."),
("You see a colleague using excessive force. What should you do?",
 "Challenge it and report it", ["Ignore it to support them", "Help them finish quickly", "Wait and raise it next week"],
 "Everyone involved has a duty of care to the person held, which includes challenging unnecessary or excessive force by colleagues."),
("How does dynamic risk assessment help during a physical intervention?",
 "It tells you when to de-escalate", ["It replaces the written incident report", "It proves the force was lawful", "It is needed before any arrest"],
 "Dynamic risk assessment informs whether, when and how to intervene, when you need help, and when to reduce force or withdraw as risks change."),
("Why is escorting someone on stairs a last resort?",
 "There is a high risk of falls", ["Stairs are rarely covered by CCTV", "It is banned under PACE", "It takes longer than the lift"],
 "Moving a resistant, ill or intoxicated person on stairs risks falls for everyone. Look for another route first and do a dynamic risk assessment."),
("Which group needs extra care because of a higher risk in physical intervention?",
 "Children and young people", ["Regular customers", "Off-duty police officers", "Large groups of friends"],
 "Vulnerable groups include children and young people, older adults and people with mental health issues. Staff who often work with them need extra training."),
("Why should physical intervention skills be kept up to date?",
 "Skills fade and guidance changes", ["The SIA retests them yearly", "Old techniques become illegal", "Venues demand new techniques monthly"],
 "Physical skills decline over time, which lowers effectiveness and raises risk, and law and best practice change. Continuing professional development keeps you safe."),
("How should a restraint be ended safely?",
 "Step down gradually while talking", ["Let go at once and walk off", "Push them away and step back", "Hold on tight until the police arrive"],
 "Disengage in a controlled way, moving to less restrictive holds and then release, explaining what is happening. In a medical emergency, release immediately."),
("After disengaging from a restraint, how can you reduce the risk of further assault?",
 "Create space between you", ["Turn your back to show trust", "Stay close to block them", "Stand within arm's reach"],
 "Create space, keep communicating with colleagues and others present and hand over safely to the police or ambulance with a full briefing."),
("What could follow from using excessive force on a customer?",
 "Prosecution and loss of your licence", ["A warning from the venue only", "Nothing if they were drunk", "A civil claim against the venue alone"],
 "Unlawful force can lead to criminal prosecution, civil claims, losing your SIA licence and your job. Intoxication does not make excessive force acceptable."),
("During a physical intervention, who do you owe a duty of care to?",
 "The person, colleagues and others", ["Only your colleagues", "Only the venue owner", "Only the person held"],
 "Your duty of care covers the person being held, during and after restraint, your colleagues and anyone else present."),
("Why should you keep talking to a person you are holding?",
 "To calm them and check welfare", ["To get a confession", "To pass the time", "To keep others away"],
 "Positive communication calms and reassures, gives clear instructions, checks understanding and wellbeing and helps manage a safe de-escalation."),
("In England and Wales, what allows a person to use reasonable force in self-defence?",
 "The common law", ["The Licensing Act 2003", "The SIA licence conditions", "The Equality Act 2010"],
 "Self-defence is a common law right, confirmed in statute by the Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008. The force must be reasonable in the circumstances as you believed them to be."),
("Two customers are fighting. What should staff aim to use to stop it?",
 "A non-aggressive team method", ["Strikes to end it quickly", "A headlock on the aggressor", "A chair as a barrier"],
 "Training covers non-aggressive methods to stop assaults or fights, both individual and team. Strikes and neck holds carry serious risk of injury."),
]
